---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const exampleUrl = 'https://github.com/gdzig/gdzig/tree/master/example';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="GDZig | Idiomatic Zig bindings for Godot 4"
  description="GDZig provides idiomatic Zig bindings for Godot 4."
>
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Godot extensions in Zig</p>
    <h1 id="hero-title">Idiomatic Zig bindings for Godot 4.</h1>
    <p>
      GDZig is under rapid development while the project shapes the API for Godot
      4.7.2 and Zig 0.16.0. Use it today with care, and expect bugs or missing
      features before a stable release.
    </p>
    <div class="actions" aria-label="Primary actions">
      <a href="/docs/" role="button">Read the docs</a>
      <a class="secondary outline" href="/api/" role="button">Browse API docs</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="audiences-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Choose your path</p>
      <h2 id="audiences-title">Start with the work you need to do.</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="grid">
      <article>
        <h3>For extension developers</h3>
        <p>
          Start here if you want to build a Godot extension in Zig. The first
          guides will cover project setup, class registration, and generated API
          lookup.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>For game developers</h3>
        <p>
          Start here if you want to understand whether GDZig fits your Godot
          project. Begin with the example project and project status notes.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="docs-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Human-written docs</p>
      <h2 id="docs-title">Use the documentation type that matches your task.</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="grid card-links">
      <article>
        <h3><a href="/docs/tutorials/">Tutorials</a></h3>
        <p>
          Guided learning paths will walk through complete GDZig projects from
          first build to Godot integration.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3><a href="/docs/how-to/">How-to guides</a></h3>
        <p>
          Task-focused guides will cover direct procedures such as building an
          extension and registering Godot-facing APIs.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3><a href="/docs/explanations/">Explanations</a></h3>
        <p>
          Concept pages will explain architecture, ownership, memory, and why
          generated API docs stay separate from guided docs.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3><a href="/docs/reference/">Reference</a></h3>
        <p>
          Curated project reference will collect supported versions, conventions,
          compatibility notes, and WebAssembly caveats.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="site-sections-title">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Project updates and examples</p>
      <h2 id="site-sections-title">Visible stubs keep future sections findable.</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="grid card-links">
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Planned</p>
        <h3><a href="/blog/">Blog</a></h3>
        <p>
          Release notes and project updates will live here. Until then, follow
          the repository for current changes.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="status-label">Planned</p>
        <h3><a href="/showcase/">Showcase</a></h3>
        <p>
          Community projects built with GDZig will appear here. Use the example
          project while this section grows.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
